vimrc: set hidden

@brief
A ticket, and the last one in this chapter.

    ORG-77  Spike: move to Neovim. Port the vimrc we actually use, and
            write down the decisions so the rest of the team does not have
            to have this argument again.

Three files are open. `:bn` and `:bp` move between them.

  1. **init.lua** — each section has one line ported already. Add the rest,
     in the style that is there.

     Options, under `vim.opt.number`:

         set ignorecase
         set smartcase
         set scrolloff=8

     Mappings, under the `<leader>w` line:

         nnoremap <leader>h :nohlsearch<CR>     desc: Clear search highlight

     Autocommands: the call at the bottom is missing its event and its
     pattern. It is the trailing-whitespace hook, for TypeScript files.

  2. **decisions.txt** — four questions, one word each, chosen from the
     options in brackets. They are judgement, not recall; the chapter has
     argued all four.

  3. **.vimrc** — the config that stays on the servers. It is open so you
     can read what you are porting from. Change it and the check fails.

Graded in vim, like everything in this chapter. No Lua runs, no Neovim
starts. What is being graded is whether you can write the config
correctly without the editor to check it for you — which, the first time
you do this for real, is exactly the situation.

@keys
| `:bn` `:bp` | next / previous buffer |
| `:wqa` | write every buffer and quit |
| `o` | open a line below — never from a comment line, in Lua or vimscript |
| `ci'` | change inside the quotes, empty pair included |

@hints
Do init.lua top to bottom, then the decisions. Search to move; the file is
short but the searches are shorter than the scrolling.
--
Open every new line from the code line above it, not from the `--` header.
`o` on a comment line gives you a comment leader, and so does Enter at the
end of one — the expected file has no `--` you did not type.
--
The autocommand blanks are two empty quote pairs. `ci'` on the first,
then `2j`, then `0` before the second `ci'` — `j` keeps the column and
`ci'` only looks forward on the line.
--
Finish with `:wqa`. `:wq` with three files in the list raises E173 and
leaves you where you started.

@solution
    /vim.opt.number<Enter> o
    vim.opt.ignorecase = true<Enter>
    vim.opt.smartcase = true<Enter>
    vim.opt.scrolloff = 8<Esc>
    /leader>w<Enter> o
    vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })<Esc>
    /create_autocmd<Enter> ci'BufWritePre<Esc> 2j 0 ci'*.ts<Esc>
    :bn<Enter>
    /editor_on<Enter> Avim<Esc> 4jAlsp<Esc> 4jAkickstart<Esc> 4jAinit.vim<Esc>
    :wqa<Enter>

About 290 keystrokes, most of them the config itself.

The four decisions, and why:

- **On servers, vim.** This is the one that is not a preference. Neovim is
  not installed on most boxes you will ssh into, and it is not your call
  whether it gets installed. Everything this dojo taught you is in the
  vim that is already there, and the day you need to fix a config file on
  a machine at 2am is the day that matters. Stay fluent in plain vim
  regardless of what you run locally.
- **LSP is the reason to switch.** Not the colours, not the floating
  windows. If you work on a large typed codebase, project-wide rename and
  real go-to-definition are worth an evening of porting. If you mostly
  edit config files, YAML and prose, the honest answer is that Neovim will
  not make your day better and vim will not hold you back.
- **Start from kickstart, not a distro.** You have written your own vimrc.
  That makes a 400-line commented `init.lua` you can read end to end a
  better fit than a framework whose config you would be configuring rather
  than writing. Distros are a fine way to *see* what modern Neovim feels
  like; they are a poor way to learn what your editor is doing.
- **Tonight, `init.vim`.** The compatibility path costs nothing and works
  immediately. Port to Lua a section at a time, over weeks, with
  `vim.cmd([[...]])` holding the parts you have not got to. A migration
  you can stop halfway through is a migration you will actually finish.

One last thing, and then the chapter is done. Nothing in chapters 1 to 30
was provisional. Not one motion, operator, text object, macro, register or
ex command changes under Neovim. Whatever you decide here, you decided it
about configuration and tooling — and you get to change your mind later,
cheaply, because the part that took a year to learn transfers whole.

@start init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua
-- ORG-77, ported from the vimrc we actually use.

vim.g.mapleader = ' '

-- Options
vim.opt.number = true

-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>w', ':w<CR>', { desc = 'Write the file' })

-- Autocommands
local config = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('Config', { clear = true })

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('', {
  group = config,
  pattern = '',
  command = [[%s/\s\+$//e]],
})

@expected init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua
-- ORG-77, ported from the vimrc we actually use.

vim.g.mapleader = ' '

-- Options
vim.opt.number = true
vim.opt.ignorecase = true
vim.opt.smartcase = true
vim.opt.scrolloff = 8

-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>w', ':w<CR>', { desc = 'Write the file' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })

-- Autocommands
local config = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('Config', { clear = true })

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufWritePre', {
  group = config,
  pattern = '*.ts',
  command = [[%s/\s\+$//e]],
})

@start decisions.txt
# ORG-77, part two. One word each, from the options in brackets.

# You ssh into three production boxes a day.
# Which editor must stay in your fingers? (vim / nvim)
editor_on_servers=

# You maintain a large TypeScript monorepo and want project-wide rename.
# Which part of Neovim gives you that? (lsp / treesitter / floating-windows)
worth_switching_for=

# You have a 90 line vimrc you wrote yourself and two evenings to spend.
# Where do you start? (distro / kickstart)
start_from=

# You want to try Neovim tonight with the config you already have.
# Which file do you create? (init.vim / init.lua)
tonight_you_write=

@expected decisions.txt
# ORG-77, part two. One word each, from the options in brackets.

# You ssh into three production boxes a day.
# Which editor must stay in your fingers? (vim / nvim)
editor_on_servers=vim

# You maintain a large TypeScript monorepo and want project-wide rename.
# Which part of Neovim gives you that? (lsp / treesitter / floating-windows)
worth_switching_for=lsp

# You have a 90 line vimrc you wrote yourself and two evenings to spend.
# Where do you start? (distro / kickstart)
start_from=kickstart

# You want to try Neovim tonight with the config you already have.
# Which file do you create? (init.vim / init.lua)
tonight_you_write=init.vim

@start .vimrc
" The config that stays on the servers. Do not change it.
set number
set ignorecase
set smartcase
set scrolloff=8

let mapleader = " "
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>
nnoremap <leader>h :nohlsearch<CR>

augroup Config
  autocmd!
  autocmd BufWritePre *.ts %s/\s\+$//e
augroup END
